--TEST--
A `...` argument through a `callable` that names its parameters answers the signature's own return type, while the arguments stay the run-time fact the count makes them — `rule:types/callable-signature`
--FILE--
<?nvs
callable(int, string): string $join = fn (int $n, string $s): string => $s . $n;

// A `...` makes the argument count the spread subject's own run-time length,
// so no argument here has a parameter to be checked against and
// `nvs_runtime::callable`'s per-argument tag check is what they are finally
// held to. What the callee answers with does not depend on its arguments,
// though, so the call reads the signature's declared return type and a
// `string` slot takes it with no conversion written.
array<mixed> $args = [7, "row "];
string $out = $join(...$args);
echo $out, "\n";

// A spread beside a written argument is the same call: still no parameter list
// to match against, still the declared `string`.
array<mixed> $tail = ["seat "];
echo $join(3, ...$tail), "\n";
--EXPECT--
row 7
seat 3
